  <h2 id="shipped-in-v0-1">Implemented in the reviewed source</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/changelog/">changelog</a> records changes. Check the
    actual installed version and help before relying on this source summary.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>CLI commands</strong> — <code>init</code>,{" "}
      <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>doctor</code>,{" "}
      <code>exec</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>import</code>,{" "}
      <code>scan</code>, <code>clone</code>, <code>info</code>,{" "}
      <code>deps</code>, <code>projects</code>, <code>sync</code>,{" "}
      <code>upgrade</code>, <code>login</code>, <code>status</code>,{" "}
      <code>open</code>, <code>ci</code>, <code>completion</code>, plus the
      interactive picker and command-specific <code>--dryRun</code> and
      <code>--json</code> options; not every command supports every option.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>39 curated providers</strong> across database, deploy, cloud,
      analytics, observability, feature flags, errors, AI, payments, code, tickets, email, and auth
      categories. Authentication, validation, creation and attachment differ by
      adapter; catalog inclusion is not completed provider setup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>5 starter templates</strong> —{" "}
      <code>nextjs-supabase-posthog</code>, <code>nextjs-neon-vercel-sentry</code>,{" "}
      <code>cloudflare-turso-clerk</code>,{" "}
      <code>claude-agent-openai-anthropic</code>, <code>supabase-posthog-sentry-resend</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>19 MCP tools + 3 resources</strong> exposed via{" "}
      <code>ashlr-stack-mcp</code> — Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, and any
      MCP-capable clients require their own configuration, project cwd, runtime
      and actual tool discovery; resources depend on file presence.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Phantom integration</strong> — credential persistence uses the installed
      Phantom interface. <code>phm_</code> strings are not generally resolved
      by Stack; <code>stack exec</code> only forwards the command. Sync and CI
      need separate qualification.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Claude Code plugin</strong> —{" "}
      <code>/stack:recommend</code>, <code>/stack:doctor</code>,{" "}
      <code>/stack:apply</code> are three of six source command files. The other
      commands are <code>/stack:add</code>, <code>/stack:list</code> and
      <code>/stack:status</code>. Check the installed plugin interface separately.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CI availability</strong> — <code>stack ci init</code> preserves its config and existing-file guards, then refuses generation with exit 1 before creating a workflow directory or writing a file. <code>stack ci init --force</code> still refuses and does not overwrite. Unattended Cloud pull is unsupported by Phantom Secrets 0.7.9; this source does not provide a CI health workflow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cross-project registry</strong> — <code>~/.stack/projects.json</code>
      records known local <code>.stack.toml</code> registrations;{" "}
      <code>stack doctor --all</code> covers those entries and available checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Source test suites</strong> cover core, CLI and MCP behavior.
      Results must be tied to the exact tested commit; they are not live-provider acceptance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Marketing + docs surface</strong> at{" "}
      <a href="https://stack.ashlr.ai">stack.ashlr.ai</a> —
      homepage, docs pages, sitemap, <code>llms.txt</code> + <code>llms-full.txt</code>,
      JSON-LD schemas.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In progress</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OAuth app registrations</strong> — broader provider auth is a goal, not an accepted
      app-registration timeline. Labels such as <code>authKind: "pat"</code>
      and <code>"oauth_pkce"</code> do not prove an available credential flow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Phantom Cloud team sync</strong> — requires its own product design and acceptance.
      Shared-secret, RBAC and audit requirements are not established by Stack source.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>More providers</strong> — coverage and new adapters remain incremental work.
      Some names already have credential-attachment adapters; a catalog entry
      does not imply complete upstream creation. No delivery cadence is promised.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windows support</strong> — must be checked against the exact package/runtime
      and platform test evidence. Historical WSL examples or a Bun-compatible
      source file do not qualify a native Windows installation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Improved error messages</strong> — proposed work to explain failures with a{" "}
      <em>what happened · why · what to do</em> triplet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Battle-testing</strong> — offline tests and source reviews are separate
      from genuine provider and installed-consumer acceptance. Those workflows
      must be qualified before a production-readiness claim.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Considering</h2>
  <p>
    The following are proposed directions, not implemented commands or
    accepted customer requirements. They are not committed to a version.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Template registry</strong> — install community templates with{" "}
      <code>stack init --template github.com/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</code>.
      This is a proposed syntax, not a supported URL-template command;
      trust and installation would require separate design.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opt-in anonymous telemetry</strong> — <em>only</em> if we can
      publish a live dashboard of every event we emit, and every event is
      defensible. Current telemetry is opt-in; future policy is a separate decision.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Provider plugins</strong> — a third-party publishing surface so
      providers we don't curate can ship as standalone packages. Today the
      39-provider list is hand-curated; a plugin API would let us stop being
      the bottleneck.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Upstream resource migration</strong> — today Stack can
      provision new resources but not migrate existing ones between
      providers (e.g. Neon → Supabase). Scoped, not started.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>TUI dashboard</strong> — <code>stack dash</code> as a Bubble
      Tea-style full-screen monitor: provider status, MCP server health,
      Phantom Cloud sync state. Nice-to-have, not load-bearing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>`stack costs`</strong> — surface each provider's current usage
      + spend via their billing APIs, rolled up per project.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Explicitly not doing</h2>
  <p>
    The honest no-list. These aren't things we haven't gotten to — they're
    things we've decided against, with reasons.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Replacing Phantom</strong> — we're not building a secret
      manager. Phantom exists, it's good, and adding a second vault would
      double the attack surface for no user benefit. If you have 1Password /
      Doppler / Infisical, we want Phantom Cloud to sync <em>to</em> them,
      not replace them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multi-cloud deploy abstractions</strong> — we're not building{" "}
      <em>another</em> layer on top of Vercel/Railway/Fly. Each deploy
      target's opinionated workflow is a feature, not a bug. Stack handles supported auth and local metadata; deployment and
      application environment acceptance remain separate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A hosted control plane</strong> — Stack is a local CLI. There
      is no stack.ashlr.ai-hosted "dashboard" coming. Your project state lives
      in <code>.stack.toml</code>; your secrets live in Phantom; everything
      else is the provider's own dashboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Yet another config format</strong> — <code>.stack.toml</code>
      is TOML because TOML is boring, readable, and already in every
      developer's mental model. No YAML, no custom DSL, no JSONC.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A UI for Phantom</strong> — Phantom ships its own TUI and web
      UI. We'd rather ship a deep integration than a shallow fork.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Replacing <code>package.json</code> / <code>Cargo.toml</code></strong>
      {" "}— Stack wires services, not language toolchains. Bun, npm, uv,
      cargo, and go own their own surfaces.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Versioning</h2>
  <p>
    Check release-specific help and migration guidance throughout the
    <code>0.x</code> period. A future <code>1.0</code> policy is not established
    by current source. The split files (<code>.stack.toml</code>,{" "}
    <code>.stack.local.toml</code>, <code>.mcp.json</code>) do not imply
    automatic migration across arbitrary future versions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Timelines on this page are <em>targets</em>, not commitments. If a thing
    is important enough to block your usage, open an issue — the roadmap
    reshuffles itself around real users.
  </p>
</Docs>
